00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.761
- Yeah, so, as you know,
"Doctor Who" follows

00:02:57.761 --> 00:03:02.182
the adventures of a
humanoid alien, basically.

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:04.362
And that person travels through space

00:03:04.362 --> 00:03:08.605
and time in a wibbly-wobbly,
timey-wimey way.

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:09.630
Yes.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.720
And so, given these facts,
what we really wanna know Brian

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.337
is travel through time
theoretically possible.

00:03:18.337 --> 00:03:21.794
At least as imagined in "Doctor Who".

00:03:21.794 --> 00:03:23.117
- You have to think about--

00:03:23.117 --> 00:03:24.655
- Oh, by the way, Brian, you know,

00:03:24.655 --> 00:03:28.963
had three best selling books
about this stuff, okay?

00:03:28.963 --> 00:03:30.663
(audience cheers)

00:03:30.663 --> 00:03:32.159
Among them, "The Fabric of the Universe",

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:36.350
"Fabric of the Cosmos",
"The Elegant Universe",

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:39.183
and, your third of those was what?

00:03:41.098 --> 00:03:41.931
- "The Hidden Reality."

00:03:41.931 --> 00:03:44.315
- "The Hidden Reality",
thank you, thank you.

00:03:44.315 --> 00:03:45.701
So go on Brian, tell me.

00:03:45.701 --> 00:03:47.350
- So you have to think about time travel

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.514
in, really two stages, because time travel

00:03:50.514 --> 00:03:51.718
to the future is very different

00:03:51.718 --> 00:03:53.897
from time travel to the past.

00:03:53.897 --> 00:03:55.307
So time travel to the future--

00:03:55.307 --> 00:03:56.140
- They have different consequences.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.245
- They have different consequences

00:03:57.245 --> 00:03:58.528
and they have a different standing

00:03:58.528 --> 00:04:00.665
with the understanding
of the laws of physics.

00:04:00.665 --> 00:04:03.493
So, as we know physics today,
time travel to the future

00:04:03.493 --> 00:04:05.918
is not hypothetical, it's not some idea

00:04:05.918 --> 00:04:08.115
that people argue about.

00:04:08.115 --> 00:04:10.398
Time travel to the
future is absolutely part

00:04:10.398 --> 00:04:12.348
of the way physics is constructed.

00:04:12.348 --> 00:04:14.040
There is nobody who knows anything about

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.351
what they're talking about
who would disagree with that.

00:04:17.351 --> 00:04:18.441
- I like the way you worded that.

00:04:18.441 --> 00:04:20.013
(Neil laughs)

00:04:20.013 --> 00:04:22.845
Okay, but of course in
"Doctor Who" they go

00:04:22.845 --> 00:04:25.049
into the past, so what's goin' on there?

00:04:25.049 --> 00:04:27.146
- That's a trickier one,
that's a tricky one.

00:04:27.146 --> 00:04:28.868
- Well he's a time lord,
so he figured it out.

00:04:28.868 --> 00:04:32.661
So, my question to you
is, in this universe,

00:04:32.661 --> 00:04:34.881
how would you make it happen such

00:04:34.881 --> 00:04:37.093
as the way we see it in "Doctor Who"?

00:04:37.093 --> 00:04:39.196
- So if you wanna travel
to the past there are

00:04:39.196 --> 00:04:41.214
a few proposals that are on the market.

00:04:41.214 --> 00:04:42.874
But I'd say the one that has attracted

00:04:42.874 --> 00:04:45.454
the greatest attention is
to make use of wormholes.

00:04:45.454 --> 00:04:46.287
- Okay.

00:04:46.287 --> 00:04:48.727
- So a wormhole is a tunnel through space,

00:04:48.727 --> 00:04:51.289
it's a shortcut, like any
ordinary tunnel in the real world.

00:04:51.289 --> 00:04:52.846
It takes it from one point in space

00:04:52.846 --> 00:04:55.584
to another point in a more direct manner

00:04:55.584 --> 00:04:56.417
than goin' all around.

00:04:56.417 --> 00:04:57.250
- So, rather than going over the mountain,

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.150
you go through the mountain.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:04:59.017
- You go right through the mountain.

00:04:59.017 --> 00:05:01.034
Now that is a tunnel from a point in space

00:05:01.034 --> 00:05:01.942
to another point in space.

00:05:01.942 --> 00:05:02.926
But here's the thing.

00:05:02.926 --> 00:05:05.008
If you take one of the
openings of the wormhole

00:05:05.008 --> 00:05:07.055
and you put it near a black hole,

00:05:07.055 --> 00:05:09.537
and you let it hang out
there, according to Einstein,

00:05:09.537 --> 00:05:12.016
time will elapse more slowly near the edge

00:05:12.016 --> 00:05:13.981
of a black hole because
of the powerful gravity.

00:05:13.981 --> 00:05:14.814
- Yes.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:16.458
- And that means that time at this opening

00:05:16.458 --> 00:05:18.336
of the wormhole will be different

00:05:18.336 --> 00:05:20.199
from time at the other
opening of the wormhole.

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:21.598
- Which is farther away
from the black hole.

00:05:21.598 --> 00:05:22.873
- Farther away from the black hole,

00:05:22.873 --> 00:05:24.851
feeling a different gravitational force.

00:05:24.851 --> 00:05:25.684
- Yes.

00:05:25.684 --> 00:05:27.114
- Which mean now, when you go through

00:05:27.114 --> 00:05:28.464
the wormhole, you're not
just going from one point

00:05:28.464 --> 00:05:30.448
in space to another point in space.

00:05:30.448 --> 00:05:31.996
You're going from one moment in time

00:05:31.996 --> 00:05:33.984
to a different moment in time.

00:05:33.984 --> 00:05:35.402
- Well, why does that mean
you're going backwards?

00:05:35.402 --> 00:05:37.942
- Well, one direction is forward,
one direction is backward.

00:05:37.942 --> 00:05:40.722
So, it's depending on the
direction that you go through

00:05:40.722 --> 00:05:41.713
the tunnel, it tells you the direction

00:05:41.713 --> 00:05:43.269
in time that you're traveling.

00:05:43.269 --> 00:05:44.102
- Oh.

00:05:45.452 --> 00:05:47.098
- There is another way
to travel back in time,

00:05:47.098 --> 00:05:49.415
scrap booking is a fantastic way.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:50.647
(laughing)

00:05:50.647 --> 00:05:52.269
Facebook memories.

00:05:52.269 --> 00:05:54.237
You see those video collections they do.

00:05:54.237 --> 00:05:55.107
You go right back in time,

00:05:55.107 --> 00:05:58.123
you're transported there instantly.

00:05:58.123 --> 00:06:00.185
- So, so, so what--

00:06:00.185 --> 00:06:01.018
- That's all I got.

00:06:01.018 --> 00:06:01.851
- So, what--

00:06:01.851 --> 00:06:05.006
- Oh, also, Einstein-Rosen bridges.

00:06:05.006 --> 00:06:07.707
- Of course, that's stupid,
everybody knows that.

00:06:07.707 --> 00:06:09.035
- But everyone knows that.

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:10.952
- So, what about the...

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.356
Something conjecture, the--

00:06:16.356 --> 00:06:17.910
- Chronology protection conjecture.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.439
- Chronology protection conjecture.

00:06:20.439 --> 00:06:22.149
- [Chuck] I believe I
have a condom named that.

00:06:22.149 --> 00:06:24.399
(laughing)

00:06:34.125 --> 00:06:36.753
- That's for when you're
lookin' for a good time.

00:06:36.753 --> 00:06:39.521
- All right, chronology
protection conjecture.

00:06:39.521 --> 00:06:42.718
So, that sounds like time travel backwards

00:06:42.718 --> 00:06:44.327
should not be allowed.

00:06:44.327 --> 00:06:46.929
- Yeah, this is an idea that
came from Stephen Hawking.

00:06:46.929 --> 00:06:48.215
And he--

00:06:48.215 --> 00:06:49.496
- Hawking, moment of silence.

00:06:49.496 --> 00:06:53.530
- I love how he looks at me
like I know Stephen Hawking.

00:06:53.530 --> 00:06:54.909
- Stephen Hawking.

00:06:54.909 --> 00:06:57.912
- And he asked the question,
I think all of us ask,

00:06:57.912 --> 00:06:59.953
which is, if time travel to the past

00:06:59.953 --> 00:07:03.779
is possible, where are the time travelers?

00:07:03.779 --> 00:07:06.248
- I've heard it hypothesized
that the reason why

00:07:06.248 --> 00:07:09.814
the Titanic sank is
everyone wanted to go back

00:07:09.814 --> 00:07:12.871
to the Titanic the first
day a time travel device

00:07:12.871 --> 00:07:14.774
was invented and then there were millions

00:07:14.774 --> 00:07:18.495
of people crammed on the
Titanic, and then it sank.

00:07:18.495 --> 00:07:21.971
- Why would you go
teleport to a sinking ship?

00:07:21.971 --> 00:07:22.988
- No, 'cause they wanted--

00:07:22.988 --> 00:07:23.821
- That's a terrible idea.

00:07:23.821 --> 00:07:25.331
- They wanted to experience
a bit of history.

00:07:25.331 --> 00:07:28.494
And, some of you may remember,
old-timer, I'm an old-timer.

00:07:28.494 --> 00:07:30.975
The very first episode of "Time Tunnel",

00:07:30.975 --> 00:07:33.483
they went back to the Titanic.

00:07:33.483 --> 00:07:35.045
Mmmm.

00:07:35.045 --> 00:07:37.072
Okay, five of you are over 50 in the room.

00:07:37.072 --> 00:07:37.905
- I was gonna say.

00:07:37.905 --> 00:07:38.896
- Remember it.

00:07:38.896 --> 00:07:40.101
- It's like either they're old,

00:07:40.101 --> 00:07:43.184
or your geek level is off the charts,

00:07:44.103 --> 00:07:45.024
that's super cool.

00:07:45.024 --> 00:07:47.281
- [Audience Member] Why not both?

00:07:47.281 --> 00:07:50.260
- Did he say why not both?

00:07:50.260 --> 00:07:53.107
I stand corrected, you old geek.

00:07:53.107 --> 00:07:55.357
(laughing)

00:07:59.786 --> 00:08:03.278
- So, Brian, so give me
an example of the problem.

00:08:03.278 --> 00:08:04.111
- Well--

00:08:04.111 --> 00:08:05.361
- [Neil] A demonstration of why

00:08:05.361 --> 00:08:06.549
that conjecture might be true.

00:08:06.549 --> 00:08:09.228
- Well, the puzzle is, if you go to

00:08:09.228 --> 00:08:11.316
the past, you change things that prevent,

00:08:11.316 --> 00:08:12.409
for instance, your own birth.

00:08:12.409 --> 00:08:14.578
How were you there in the first
place to make that change?

00:08:14.578 --> 00:08:16.893
So, the usual example
we talk about, you see

00:08:16.893 --> 00:08:19.270
it in "Back to the Future",
many other incarnations

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:20.729
of these ideas.

00:08:20.729 --> 00:08:22.706
You go back to the past and
you prevent your parents

00:08:22.706 --> 00:08:24.184
from meeting, right?

00:08:24.184 --> 00:08:27.085
So, how were you there
to execute that action?

00:08:27.085 --> 00:08:29.362
If your parents never met,
so you were never born.

00:08:29.362 --> 00:08:31.770
So you run into this logical paradox

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.871
if you allow certain kinds of changes

00:08:33.871 --> 00:08:35.767
to the past to take place.

00:08:35.767 --> 00:08:37.552
Now there are ways out of this.

00:08:37.552 --> 00:08:39.315
So you don't need the chronology
protection conjecture.

00:08:39.315 --> 00:08:41.873
Which would say you
can't travel to the past.

00:08:41.873 --> 00:08:43.550
You can simply have it that the laws

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:46.975
of physics prevent you
from ever changing anything

00:08:46.975 --> 00:08:48.184
that was in the past.

00:08:48.184 --> 00:08:50.447
Namely, if you go to the
past, you were always

00:08:50.447 --> 00:08:52.073
at that moment, there was never

00:08:52.073 --> 00:08:54.954
a version of that moment
when you were not there.

00:08:54.954 --> 00:08:57.280
So it's always self
consistent because moments

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:59.883
of time just are, they can't change.

00:08:59.883 --> 00:09:02.741
- Could it be, for example,
let's say I figure out how

00:09:02.741 --> 00:09:06.397
to send tachyons in a text message.

00:09:06.397 --> 00:09:08.150
These are the hypothetical particles

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:09.719
that would travel backwards in time.

00:09:09.719 --> 00:09:11.313
I see you walk, you're
a good friend of mine,

00:09:11.313 --> 00:09:12.435
you're walking down the street,

00:09:12.435 --> 00:09:14.120
and you slip on a banana peel.

00:09:14.120 --> 00:09:15.999
And I say, "Oh, let me warn my friend

00:09:15.999 --> 00:09:17.891
"that he's about to
slip on a banana peel."

00:09:17.891 --> 00:09:21.289
So I quickly pull out my
tachyon texting device

00:09:21.289 --> 00:09:22.122
and I say--

00:09:22.122 --> 00:09:24.005
- You're a better friend than
I am, that's all I'm saying.

00:09:24.005 --> 00:09:25.209
(laughing)

00:09:25.209 --> 00:09:26.571
'Cause that's hilarious,

00:09:26.571 --> 00:09:29.084
and I'm not warning you of anything.

00:09:29.084 --> 00:09:31.810
- Oh my god, can you sext through time?

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:32.643
That'd be amazing.

00:09:32.643 --> 00:09:34.102
(laughing)

00:09:34.102 --> 00:09:35.044
- So as I send the text, I say,

00:09:35.044 --> 00:09:37.572
"Brian, watch out for the banana peel."

00:09:37.572 --> 00:09:39.103
So then it goes backwards in time.

00:09:39.103 --> 00:09:41.260
So then are you suggesting
what might happen is,

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:44.333
you're walking down the lane

00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:47.090
and then I send you a text and--

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:48.381
(James mimics phone ringing)

00:09:48.381 --> 00:09:49.214
Thank you.

00:09:49.214 --> 00:09:51.069
And then you pick it
up to read it, it says,

00:09:51.069 --> 00:09:53.237
"Watch out for the banana peel."

00:09:53.237 --> 00:09:54.762
And you slip on the banana peel

00:09:54.762 --> 00:09:56.103
because you were reading my text.

00:09:56.103 --> 00:09:58.934
- [Brian] Yes, exactly right.

00:09:58.934 --> 00:10:01.024
- So, what you're saying
is, you can't change,

00:10:01.024 --> 00:10:04.042
because no matter what,
you were there, period.

00:10:04.042 --> 00:10:06.413
Whatever that moment in time
was, you were already there.

00:10:06.413 --> 00:10:07.836
- [Neil] He fell on his
ass, no matter what.

00:10:07.836 --> 00:10:08.993
- Or the influence was there.

00:10:08.993 --> 00:10:09.980
- Or the influence was there.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:10.813
- Yeah, yeah, exactly, yeah.

00:10:10.813 --> 00:10:12.193
- Is there another possibility
that when you go back

00:10:12.193 --> 00:10:13.831
in time, and if you change something,

00:10:13.831 --> 00:10:16.054
it splinters off to its
own different universe?

00:10:16.054 --> 00:10:18.177
- [Brian] That's the other
possibility, yes, thank you.

00:10:18.177 --> 00:10:19.917
- That's mind blowing stuff.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:10:19.917 --> 00:10:21.945
- [Brian] So, the other idea

00:10:21.945 --> 00:10:23.827
is that you can change the past--

00:10:23.827 --> 00:10:25.542
- I just fist pounded
with Niel deGrasse Tyson!

00:10:25.542 --> 00:10:27.558
(audience cheers)

00:10:27.558 --> 00:10:28.975
Sorry, NDT, baby!

00:10:30.514 --> 00:10:32.283
- So, you can change the past,

00:10:32.283 --> 00:10:34.983
but you can't change the
past of your own universe.

00:10:34.983 --> 00:10:36.721
So if you go back into the past

00:10:36.721 --> 00:10:38.655
and say you prevent your
parents from meeting.

00:10:38.655 --> 00:10:40.055
You're preventing them from meeting

00:10:40.055 --> 00:10:41.912
in a parallel copy of the universe

00:10:41.912 --> 00:10:43.571
within which you were born.

00:10:43.571 --> 00:10:46.251
So, if they don't meet,
that means you won't be born

00:10:46.251 --> 00:10:47.665
in that universe.
- In that universe.

00:10:47.665 --> 00:10:49.842
- But there's no mystery
of where you came from.

00:10:49.842 --> 00:10:52.600
You were born in this universe
where your parents did meet.

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:54.491
- See, but I have a
whole different theory.

00:10:54.491 --> 00:10:57.094
You go back in time to keep
your parents from meeting.

00:10:57.094 --> 00:10:59.645
But, unfortunately, you
get there just too late,

00:10:59.645 --> 00:11:01.193
and instead, you actually see

00:11:01.193 --> 00:11:03.188
the moment of your own conception.

00:11:03.188 --> 00:11:04.900
(laughing)

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:07.221
And then, because you witnessed that,

00:11:07.221 --> 00:11:09.779
you take out a gun and kill yourself.

00:11:09.779 --> 00:11:12.029
(laughing)

00:11:17.266 --> 00:11:19.331
Everybody changing the timeline.

00:11:19.331 --> 00:11:20.692
- Everyone here is now thinking

00:11:20.692 --> 00:11:22.290
about their parents doing it.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:23.273
Everyone.

00:11:23.273 --> 00:11:24.995
(laughing)

00:11:24.995 --> 00:11:27.250
- So, were you a fan of
the "Doctor Who" series?

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:29.744
- I've actually only seen one episode.

00:11:29.744 --> 00:11:30.577
- What?

00:11:32.027 --> 00:11:34.826
Security, could you--
- I'll leave, goodnight guys.

00:11:34.826 --> 00:11:35.659
(laughing)

00:11:35.659 --> 00:11:37.036
- That is ridiculous,
'cause I've never seen

00:11:37.036 --> 00:11:39.055
a single episode.

00:11:39.055 --> 00:11:39.888
So sorry.

00:11:39.888 --> 00:11:40.805
- Security.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:45.181
All right, so let me ask, what episode?

00:11:45.181 --> 00:11:47.006
- [Chuck] Of course, everybody now wants

00:11:47.006 --> 00:11:47.983
to know what episode?

00:11:47.983 --> 00:11:48.816
- Right.

00:11:48.816 --> 00:11:53.519
- I don't know, there was
this blue police thingamajigy.

00:11:53.519 --> 00:11:54.976
(audience laughs)

00:11:54.976 --> 00:11:57.490
And that's all I
remember, sorry that's it.

00:11:57.490 --> 00:11:58.458
- Does that clear it
- Security could we

00:11:58.458 --> 00:11:59.668
take him out here?
- Up for ya?

00:11:59.668 --> 00:12:01.467
Right, 'cause guess what,
as she went just like this,

00:12:01.467 --> 00:12:03.179
"That was episode 961."

00:12:03.179 --> 00:12:04.675
(laughing)

00:12:04.675 --> 00:12:07.549
- The one with the blue
thingy, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:12:07.549 --> 00:12:11.804
So, one of the interesting
features of "Doctor Who"

00:12:11.804 --> 00:12:16.367
is that this "blue thingy",
it's called a TARDIS.

00:12:16.367 --> 00:12:18.680
(audience cheers)

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:21.181
Which is an acronym for

00:12:21.181 --> 00:12:24.545
Time And Relative Dimension In Space.

00:12:24.545 --> 00:12:26.571
- Okay, by the way.

00:12:26.571 --> 00:12:27.404
- Yeah.

00:12:27.404 --> 00:12:28.992
- By the way, I gotta say,

00:12:28.992 --> 00:12:31.490
that was pretty damn
awesome what I saw here.

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:35.480
You just went, "By the way
it's an acronym, stands for."

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:39.290
And everybody else just said
it right along with you.

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:40.987
- 'Cause that's how we roll at Comic Con.

00:12:40.987 --> 00:12:42.453
- They just said it
without missin' a beat.

00:12:42.453 --> 00:12:44.688
(audience cheers)

00:12:44.688 --> 00:12:46.970
- So one of the
fascinating elements of it,

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:48.921
and they just treated this quite casually

00:12:48.921 --> 00:12:49.990
throughout the series.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.173
Is that you walk into
this police call box,

00:12:52.173 --> 00:12:53.852
it's a British police call box.

00:12:53.852 --> 00:12:56.516
And then inside is the entire ship.

00:12:56.516 --> 00:12:59.654
So, you're accessing another dimension

00:12:59.654 --> 00:13:02.046
of space by entering in it.

00:13:02.046 --> 00:13:03.668
So this would be like a fourth dimension.

00:13:03.668 --> 00:13:04.501
- Yeah.

00:13:04.501 --> 00:13:05.334
So you're saying the inside

00:13:05.334 --> 00:13:06.412
is bigger than the outside.
- Bigger than the outside.

00:13:06.412 --> 00:13:07.392
- You did say it right.

00:13:07.392 --> 00:13:09.379
- That's what I keep tellin' her!

00:13:09.379 --> 00:13:10.212
Wait, what?

00:13:10.212 --> 00:13:11.045
- As if to say...

00:13:11.045 --> 00:13:11.878
(laughing)

00:13:11.878 --> 00:13:13.528
- I don't know if it works.

00:13:13.528 --> 00:13:14.589
I don't know if the joke works.

00:13:14.589 --> 00:13:17.332
- You just have to say, the phrase is,

00:13:17.332 --> 00:13:18.681
"It's bigger on the inside."

00:13:18.681 --> 00:13:19.577
That's all you gotta say.

00:13:19.577 --> 00:13:20.861
Repeat that.

00:13:20.861 --> 00:13:21.861
Just repeat it.

00:13:21.861 --> 00:13:22.827
- I'm afraid to.

00:13:22.827 --> 00:13:24.955
(laughing)

00:13:24.955 --> 00:13:26.452
- So, "Doctor Who" has just

00:13:26.452 --> 00:13:29.147
been very smartly written in this regard.

00:13:29.147 --> 00:13:32.392
It's got good physics in
it, and yes it's fiction,

00:13:32.392 --> 00:13:36.023
but it's got you thinking about how events

00:13:36.023 --> 00:13:39.543
can influence for past and future.

00:13:39.543 --> 00:13:41.985
And this is why they say, time

00:13:41.985 --> 00:13:45.226
is this wibbly-wobbly, timey-wimey.

00:13:45.226 --> 00:13:47.287
It's not so rigid that it's
gonna follow what you think

00:13:47.287 --> 00:13:50.230
is a strict law of physics.

00:13:50.230 --> 00:13:52.802
It's got some more, sort
of, chaotic elements to it.

00:13:52.802 --> 00:13:54.051
- But it has a single timeline,

00:13:54.051 --> 00:13:55.322
or it has multiple timelines?

00:13:55.322 --> 00:13:57.781
- I interpret it as multiple timelines.

00:13:57.781 --> 00:13:58.765
- Okay.

00:13:58.765 --> 00:14:01.274
- And the time-lord
can access any timeline

00:14:01.274 --> 00:14:03.193
that is necessary for the task at hand.

00:14:03.193 --> 00:14:05.303
So that he can save the
universe in every episode.

00:14:05.303 --> 00:14:06.429
- Could I ask a general question?

00:14:06.429 --> 00:14:09.027
Is time just a human construct?

00:14:09.027 --> 00:14:11.122
Do other species understand the concept

00:14:11.122 --> 00:14:15.389
of time, the passing of it,
and the future versus the past?

00:14:15.389 --> 00:14:16.703
Yes, okay, yes they do.

00:14:16.703 --> 00:14:17.536
- There you go.

00:14:17.536 --> 00:14:18.369
- They do?

00:14:18.369 --> 00:14:20.321
- I mean it's easy, it's
all too easy for we humans

00:14:20.321 --> 00:14:23.829
to say what we do and think,
that other animals do not.

00:14:23.829 --> 00:14:24.662
- [James] Yes.

00:14:24.662 --> 00:14:27.357
- And that's, there's the
human hubris deeply embedded

00:14:27.357 --> 00:14:28.840
in that assumption.

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:30.286
Who are you to say that other animals

00:14:30.286 --> 00:14:34.053
don't think, or contemplate,
or wonder about their fate.

00:14:34.053 --> 00:14:37.470
And maybe we are pitied by eagles because

00:14:38.442 --> 00:14:41.270
our vision sucks compared
to that of eagles.

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:44.518
And they look, those poor
humans, they can't even fly.

00:14:44.518 --> 00:14:48.272
I wonder if they dream
about, oh, those poor things.

00:14:48.272 --> 00:14:50.750
Do you know how much pity
we might be garnering

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.256
in the minds and bodies of other animals?

00:14:53.256 --> 00:14:55.497
- Most animals look down on me, yes.

00:14:55.497 --> 00:14:57.883
- And I got another one,
here's another one right?

00:14:57.883 --> 00:15:00.982
We can talk about and
praise certain primates.

00:15:00.982 --> 00:15:05.165
Other primates who maybe
mimic sign language, okay?

00:15:05.165 --> 00:15:07.140
So, these are animals that can speak

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:10.504
to us using, typically,
American sign language.

00:15:10.504 --> 00:15:12.421
But no one is asking...

00:15:13.375 --> 00:15:18.025
Here, here, here, chimps
speaking to us in our language,

00:15:18.025 --> 00:15:19.689
but at no time are we ask, can we speak

00:15:19.689 --> 00:15:21.957
to them in their language?

00:15:21.957 --> 00:15:25.283
(audience cheers)

00:15:25.283 --> 00:15:26.854
- So, they're smart enough
to speak our language,

00:15:26.854 --> 00:15:28.353
we're not smart enough to speak theirs.

00:15:28.353 --> 00:15:31.686
So, I don't know, I'm just, I want to...

00:15:34.629 --> 00:15:38.151
- All I know is what you said
is beautiful and eloquent.

00:15:38.151 --> 00:15:40.411
But I doubt if there's
a horse somewhere going,

00:15:40.411 --> 00:15:42.544
"I wonder about the fabric of time."

00:15:42.544 --> 00:15:44.794
(laughing)

00:15:46.522 --> 00:15:49.366
- Yeah, goldfish are
not thinking about time,

00:15:49.366 --> 00:15:51.172
they're just doing circles in the bowl.

00:15:51.172 --> 00:15:53.254
They can't remember their
ass from their elbow.

00:15:53.254 --> 00:15:54.099
They have neither of those
- Yeah keep telling

00:15:54.099 --> 00:15:55.266
yourself that.

00:15:56.495 --> 00:15:59.006
So, one of Hawking's
quotes, which is related

00:15:59.006 --> 00:16:03.659
to my Titanic comment, is,
"If time travel were possible,

00:16:03.659 --> 00:16:07.100
"where are the tourists from the future?"

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:09.070
- Yeah, I think that is the question.

00:16:09.070 --> 00:16:11.720
And one answer is, every
time they come here,

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:14.415
we just put them in white
coats and lock 'em up.

00:16:14.415 --> 00:16:16.083
I mean that's one possibility, right?

00:16:16.083 --> 00:16:16.916
- Ooh.

00:16:16.916 --> 00:16:17.844
- Or we put them in white coats

00:16:17.844 --> 00:16:19.994
and call them astrophysicists.

00:16:19.994 --> 00:16:22.244
(laughing)

00:16:23.225 --> 00:16:27.392
- But wait a minute, isn't
this quote from Hawking

00:16:28.638 --> 00:16:31.888
exactly what a time traveler would say?

00:16:33.570 --> 00:16:34.804
- [Brian] Ah.

00:16:34.804 --> 00:16:36.100
- [Chuck] On that one
we just gotta meditate.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:38.319
- [Brian] I see where
you're goin' with this.

00:16:38.319 --> 00:16:41.738
- So, the people who are
way ahead of the rest of us

00:16:41.738 --> 00:16:46.080
at any given time in space,
like DaVinci, like Einstein,

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:49.386
like Marie Curie, like Stephen Hawking.

00:16:49.386 --> 00:16:50.887
- [Chuck] Or Snooki.

00:16:50.887 --> 00:16:54.044
- [James] Snooki, isn't
it temporal law that

00:16:54.044 --> 00:16:56.020
if you ask a time traveler if they're

00:16:56.020 --> 00:16:57.908
a time traveler, they have to
say they're a time traveler,

00:16:57.908 --> 00:17:01.024
like a cop or a vampire, right?

00:17:01.024 --> 00:17:03.805
- I have not heard that law, but...

00:17:03.805 --> 00:17:05.862
- Temporal prime directive, no?

00:17:05.862 --> 00:17:10.612
- So, what is the casual loop
paradox, is that somethin'?

00:17:12.209 --> 00:17:14.678
I just learned about this.
- Causal loop paradox, maybe?

00:17:14.678 --> 00:17:16.273
- Sorry, sorry, causal
loop paradox, what is that?

00:17:16.273 --> 00:17:19.209
- Yeah, so, you don't wanna have loops

00:17:19.209 --> 00:17:23.990
in space and time in which you
can't have cause and effect

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:26.268
be logically consistent, right?

00:17:26.268 --> 00:17:27.669
So, we're all used to things in

00:17:27.669 --> 00:17:29.398
the past affecting things in the future.

00:17:29.398 --> 00:17:31.183
But if time loops around, then

00:17:31.183 --> 00:17:34.481
the future actually can affect the past.

00:17:34.481 --> 00:17:37.034
So, the danger in a theory, and one of

00:17:37.034 --> 00:17:39.942
the diagnostics we use to
see if a theory is healthy,

00:17:39.942 --> 00:17:41.318
is to make sure it doesn't have any

00:17:41.318 --> 00:17:43.247
of these closed time-like curves.

00:17:43.247 --> 00:17:46.321
In which cause and effect
would be muddled up

00:17:46.321 --> 00:17:47.764
by the structure space time.

00:17:47.764 --> 00:17:49.272
- This is a philosophical bias you

00:17:49.272 --> 00:17:51.190
are implementing on the universe itself.

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:52.833
- It's a little bit
more than philosophical.

00:17:52.833 --> 00:17:55.437
Because you want the unfolding of events

00:17:55.437 --> 00:17:57.548
to be internally consistent.

00:17:57.548 --> 00:17:58.720
- You're saying you want something

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:00.073
to be true in the universe.

00:18:00.073 --> 00:18:01.241
That sounds like you're
begging for something--

00:18:01.241 --> 00:18:03.944
- Let me just say, God needs
it to be that way, right?

00:18:03.944 --> 00:18:08.422
Because if the laws of physics
yield inconsistent results,

00:18:08.422 --> 00:18:10.968
then everything falls apart,
everything disintegrates.

00:18:10.968 --> 00:18:12.497
- No, we just live in an
inconsistent universe.

00:18:12.497 --> 00:18:13.330
If you have a problem with that.

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:14.274
- No, no, no, what it means

00:18:14.274 --> 00:18:16.136
is our understanding is messed up.

00:18:16.136 --> 00:18:17.918
Clearly the world makes sense.

00:18:17.918 --> 00:18:21.449
- No, not the (laughs).

00:18:21.449 --> 00:18:25.062
- I take that back, I take that back.

00:18:25.062 --> 00:18:25.895
- Oh my god.

00:18:25.895 --> 00:18:28.582
- I used to believe
that, I take it back now.

00:18:28.582 --> 00:18:29.415
- The world makes sense.

00:18:29.415 --> 00:18:30.953
- I just love that your
voice cracked like you

00:18:30.953 --> 00:18:34.237
were a teenager when you said no.

00:18:34.237 --> 00:18:36.368
Clearly, the word, "no."

00:18:36.368 --> 00:18:37.567
(laughing)

00:18:37.567 --> 00:18:39.417
All right, so do you think one day we'll

00:18:39.417 --> 00:18:40.980
have a machine that will
just send us forward in time?

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:42.978
We know it's possible, but why
aren't we doing it actively?

00:18:42.978 --> 00:18:45.734
- Well I think we do have a
machine in principle, right?

00:18:45.734 --> 00:18:48.260
We just can't go very far forward in time.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:50.823
Because every time you go into a vehicle

00:18:50.823 --> 00:18:52.925
and you undertake a roundtrip journey,

00:18:52.925 --> 00:18:55.344
you are traveling into the future.

00:18:55.344 --> 00:18:56.632
- By some calculatable amount.

00:18:56.632 --> 00:18:59.473
- Yeah, now, at ordinary
speeds you're only going

00:18:59.473 --> 00:19:01.354
a billionth of a billionth of a second,

00:19:01.354 --> 00:19:02.314
or something like that.

00:19:02.314 --> 00:19:03.147
- Into the future.

00:19:03.147 --> 00:19:04.530
- Into the future, but
if you found yourself

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:06.226
in a ship that is going
near the speed of light.

00:19:06.226 --> 00:19:07.168
You go out for six months,

00:19:07.168 --> 00:19:09.070
you turn around you come
back for six months,

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:11.346
you get off that ship,
you'll have aged one year.

00:19:11.346 --> 00:19:13.929
But depending on how close to
the speed of light you got,

00:19:13.929 --> 00:19:15.864
when you step off the ship, Earth will

00:19:15.864 --> 00:19:18.915
be 10 thousand years or a
million years into the future.

00:19:18.915 --> 00:19:21.278
You will have jumped into Earth's future.

00:19:21.278 --> 00:19:23.844
And that is what we mean by time travel.

00:19:23.844 --> 00:19:25.149
- How important, in the future,

00:19:25.149 --> 00:19:28.555
are the survival of humpback whales?

00:19:28.555 --> 00:19:30.167
(laughing)

00:19:30.167 --> 00:19:31.515
- [Neil] "Star Trek" four.

00:19:31.515 --> 00:19:33.018
(audience cheers)

00:19:33.018 --> 00:19:34.066
- Right, because it seems
- The movie.

00:19:34.066 --> 00:19:36.592
- Like we've gotta solve the
humpback whale problem, stat.

00:19:36.592 --> 00:19:38.430
- [Neil] You just need transparent
aluminum and then you--

00:19:38.430 --> 00:19:39.305
- That's it, that's it.

00:19:39.305 --> 00:19:43.261
- [Neil] Yeah (laughs).

00:19:43.261 --> 00:19:45.911
- But, the faster you go
to the speed of light,

00:19:45.911 --> 00:19:47.955
the slower time moves, correct?

00:19:47.955 --> 00:19:50.395
So, somebody on a
spaceship would age slower

00:19:50.395 --> 00:19:51.375
than somebody on Earth.

00:19:51.375 --> 00:19:53.017
They'd come back one year later and

00:19:53.017 --> 00:19:55.110
the people on Earth have
aged 10 years, 100 years,

00:19:55.110 --> 00:19:55.943
something like that.

00:19:55.943 --> 00:19:57.114
That's the basic idea?

00:19:57.114 --> 00:19:58.068
- That's the idea.

00:19:58.068 --> 00:20:00.348
- And let me add, just to
close out this segment.

00:20:00.348 --> 00:20:03.930
Our GPS satellites orbit
high enough, so that there's

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:05.748
a measurable and important difference

00:20:05.748 --> 00:20:08.192
in its rate of time that it keeps,

00:20:08.192 --> 00:20:10.571
compared to us here on
the surface of the Earth.

00:20:10.571 --> 00:20:12.010
They're farther away from the source

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:15.184
of gravity Earth, so
their time ticks faster

00:20:15.184 --> 00:20:17.039
than our clock time on the surface.

00:20:17.039 --> 00:20:17.993
And we know this

00:20:17.993 --> 00:20:19.913
because Einstein's general
theory of relativity.

00:20:19.913 --> 00:20:23.232
So, they calculated how much
faster the clocks would tick,

00:20:23.232 --> 00:20:27.143
and they correct for
that before it sends us

00:20:27.143 --> 00:20:29.282
the time that ends up on our smart phone.

00:20:29.282 --> 00:20:31.082
- So if you accelerate
towards the speed of light,

00:20:31.082 --> 00:20:32.355
time actually slows down.

00:20:32.355 --> 00:20:33.188
- Yes.

00:20:33.188 --> 00:20:35.023
- Okay, hold on.

00:20:35.023 --> 00:20:37.273
(laughing)

00:20:39.741 --> 00:20:42.717
Chuck, sell Apple, sell your Apple!

00:20:42.717 --> 00:20:44.965
(laughing)

00:20:44.965 --> 00:20:47.791
- Thanks everybody for
coming to the 2019 Comic Con.

00:20:47.791 --> 00:20:49.027
(laughing)

00:20:49.027 --> 00:20:51.656
You've been gone a year, Chuck.

00:20:51.656 --> 00:20:53.331
- All right, we got a break
time travel to our close.

00:20:53.331 --> 00:20:54.355
Thank you for that topic.

00:20:54.355 --> 00:20:59.355
Comic Con, our next topic is
the quantum realm of Ant-Man.

00:20:59.973 --> 00:21:01.684
Give it up for my panel for that.

00:21:01.684 --> 00:21:04.517
(audience cheers)

00:21:05.553 --> 00:21:08.033
I've got with me theoretical
physicist, Brian Greene.

00:21:08.033 --> 00:21:10.179
Brian, that, you can applaud, yes.

00:21:10.179 --> 00:21:13.287
(audience cheers)

00:21:13.287 --> 00:21:15.581
I got Chuck Nice, my co-host, Chuck.

00:21:15.581 --> 00:21:17.264
(audience cheers)

00:21:17.264 --> 00:21:18.783
Chuck, who did you bring?

00:21:18.783 --> 00:21:23.616
- And of course, from "Impractical
Jokers", it's the Murr!

00:21:24.552 --> 00:21:26.562
- [James] Hi everybody.

00:21:26.562 --> 00:21:28.240
- The mole.

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:30.320
Alright, so in a recent movie,

00:21:30.320 --> 00:21:33.199
"The Ant-Man and the Wasp", okay.

00:21:33.199 --> 00:21:35.231
That's starring Paul Rudd, who we've

00:21:35.231 --> 00:21:37.366
had on Star Talk live before.

00:21:37.366 --> 00:21:38.949
- He is delightful.

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:44.543
- So, Brian, what does physics
say about shrinking things?

00:21:44.543 --> 00:21:47.671
- Well, it's tough to do, right?

00:21:47.671 --> 00:21:49.799
Because ultimately, things
are made of molecules

00:21:49.799 --> 00:21:52.481
and atoms and the quantum laws restrict

00:21:52.481 --> 00:21:54.803
the size of the individual atom.

00:21:54.803 --> 00:21:57.030
So, it's pretty tough to imagine changing

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:59.709
them without changing the
basic constants of nature.

00:21:59.709 --> 00:22:02.012
- Wait, wait, but Rutherford,
a hundred years ago,

00:22:02.012 --> 00:22:04.690
noticed that atoms are mostly empty space.

00:22:04.690 --> 00:22:05.523
- They are.

00:22:05.523 --> 00:22:06.602
- Why can't you just
squeeze that puppy down?

00:22:06.602 --> 00:22:07.743
- Yeah.

00:22:07.743 --> 00:22:10.853
Yeah, in fact if you were to squeeze down,

00:22:10.853 --> 00:22:13.890
if you could do it, the space in the atoms

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:16.371
in every single human
being that has ever lived

00:22:16.371 --> 00:22:18.567
on planet Earth, the resulting stuff

00:22:18.567 --> 00:22:20.135
would fit inside of a baseball.

00:22:20.135 --> 00:22:21.083
- [James] Wow.

00:22:21.083 --> 00:22:21.963
- Ooh.

00:22:21.963 --> 00:22:23.746
- [James] And that's
even with the, you know,

00:22:23.746 --> 00:22:24.579
obesity problem in America?

00:22:24.579 --> 00:22:26.917
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:22:26.917 --> 00:22:28.282
No, that's, softball then.

00:22:28.282 --> 00:22:29.115
- Softball, yeah.

00:22:29.115 --> 00:22:31.365
(laughing)

00:22:33.513 --> 00:22:35.612
- So, why is there some challenge

00:22:35.612 --> 00:22:37.914
then, to shrinking out that empty space.

00:22:37.914 --> 00:22:39.651
- It's very hard to get
that empty space out

00:22:39.651 --> 00:22:43.728
because atoms have a
certain amount of energy.

00:22:43.728 --> 00:22:45.278
And whereas we're used to being able

00:22:45.278 --> 00:22:47.696
to continuously change the
energy in something, right?

00:22:47.696 --> 00:22:49.861
If I take this from down on
the floor, this water bottle,

00:22:49.861 --> 00:22:51.772
and as I raise it up higher and higher,

00:22:51.772 --> 00:22:54.479
it looks like I'm
continuously adding in energy.

00:22:54.479 --> 00:22:55.312
- Yeah.

00:22:55.312 --> 00:22:57.215
- Quantum mechanics
says that is misleading.

00:22:57.215 --> 00:22:58.951
Energy comes in steps.

00:22:58.951 --> 00:23:01.047
You can't actually continuously change it.

00:23:01.047 --> 00:23:02.339
So, for an atom, when you get down

00:23:02.339 --> 00:23:04.047
to the lowest step, the lowest energy,

00:23:04.047 --> 00:23:07.762
there is no lower step
that is in existence.

00:23:07.762 --> 00:23:09.840
So, if you have a
minimum amount of energy,

00:23:09.840 --> 00:23:12.624
you can't actually squeeze
things down to zero size.

00:23:12.624 --> 00:23:14.858
- You telling me that we
can't have Ant-Man then?

00:23:14.858 --> 00:23:16.637
- Well you can't have Ant-Man,
that's sort of the point.

00:23:16.637 --> 00:23:18.442
- All right, so, let me ask you this.

00:23:18.442 --> 00:23:22.907
What constant of nature
would need to be adjusted

00:23:22.907 --> 00:23:24.522
so that you could shrink down the atom?

00:23:24.522 --> 00:23:25.585
Is it Planck's constant?

00:23:25.585 --> 00:23:26.962
- Yeah, Planck constant, one of them.

00:23:26.962 --> 00:23:29.139
So, that's a, to be a little bit,

00:23:29.139 --> 00:23:31.298
that's a dimensional
number, but the radius

00:23:31.298 --> 00:23:33.476
of the atom depends on Planck's constant.

00:23:33.476 --> 00:23:35.085
It depends on the mass of the electron.

00:23:35.085 --> 00:23:37.866
It depends on the electric
charge of the electron.

00:23:37.866 --> 00:23:39.039
So if you change those numbers
- So if you could imagine

00:23:39.039 --> 00:23:42.789
a zone where you influence
quantum constants,

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.843
you could, in principle
make a much smaller version

00:23:45.843 --> 00:23:47.396
of what existed in a larger state.

00:23:47.396 --> 00:23:49.449
- You could, now you'd
probably destroy the universe,

00:23:49.449 --> 00:23:50.712
but yes you could probably do that.

00:23:50.712 --> 00:23:51.545
- That's all right, we'll take that risk.

00:23:51.545 --> 00:23:52.378
- [Chuck] Well, but
it'd be worth it though.

00:23:52.378 --> 00:23:53.211
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:23:53.211 --> 00:23:54.044
- That'd be worth it, totally worth it!

00:23:54.044 --> 00:23:54.897
- Yeah, just to make it happen.

00:23:54.897 --> 00:23:56.552
- How would it destroy the universe?

00:23:56.552 --> 00:23:57.385
- What's that?

00:23:57.385 --> 00:23:58.218
- How would it destroy the universe?

00:23:58.218 --> 00:24:01.000
- Well, the universe depends on the values

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:04.485
of those constants in a very
specific and delicate way.

00:24:04.485 --> 00:24:05.461
You start playing this game--

00:24:05.461 --> 00:24:08.066
- The speed of light, the
gravitational constant.

00:24:08.066 --> 00:24:09.541
- Yeah, you change the
ratio of the masses,

00:24:09.541 --> 00:24:11.145
you change the strengths of the forces,

00:24:11.145 --> 00:24:13.095
you change the fundamental speeds.

00:24:13.095 --> 00:24:16.004
And, for instance stars no
longer have nuclear processes

00:24:16.004 --> 00:24:17.835
and stars don't light up, without stars

00:24:17.835 --> 00:24:19.317
the universe is a very different place.

00:24:19.317 --> 00:24:22.068
So, in that sense, you don't
just change the constants

00:24:22.068 --> 00:24:24.705
and look at how a bottle of water changes.

00:24:24.705 --> 00:24:27.055
You see how the entire universe changes.

00:24:27.055 --> 00:24:28.075
- No, that's if you change the constants

00:24:28.075 --> 00:24:29.269
for the entire universe.

00:24:29.269 --> 00:24:30.225
I'm talking about changing the constants

00:24:30.225 --> 00:24:31.954
for just where Paul Rudd is standing.

00:24:31.954 --> 00:24:34.033
- Okay, well yeah, yeah.

00:24:34.033 --> 00:24:35.829
In principle you could imagine that

00:24:35.829 --> 00:24:37.627
in your wildest dreams, but yeah, yeah.

00:24:37.627 --> 00:24:38.665
- [Neil] Yeah, that's what we do here,

00:24:38.665 --> 00:24:39.881
we imagine our wildest dreams.
- Let's do it, let's do it,

00:24:39.881 --> 00:24:40.714
let's do it.

00:24:40.714 --> 00:24:41.547
- That's not even my wildest dreams.

00:24:41.547 --> 00:24:44.010
I live in a world where a reality TV star

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:45.540
is the president, okay?

00:24:45.540 --> 00:24:47.790
(laughing)

00:24:49.455 --> 00:24:53.129
- Well, he's overinflated isn't he?

00:24:53.129 --> 00:24:55.379
(laughing)

00:24:56.431 --> 00:24:57.937
- Where am I here?

00:24:57.937 --> 00:24:59.930
- But yeah, in terms of
shrinking, yes, if you could--

00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:01.538
- Okay, so, now watch what happens.

00:25:01.538 --> 00:25:02.875
So, if you shrink.

00:25:02.875 --> 00:25:05.890
Now I made you smaller, but
you still have the same mass.

00:25:05.890 --> 00:25:10.473
And in the movie, Ant-Man is
riding the back of an ant.

00:25:11.832 --> 00:25:15.099
Now, he would still weigh
his 160 pounds, 170 pounds

00:25:15.099 --> 00:25:16.935
on the back of an ant,
but he'd be this big.

00:25:16.935 --> 00:25:18.021
He'd squash the ant.

00:25:18.021 --> 00:25:18.854
- Yeah.

00:25:18.854 --> 00:25:20.602
- So, is there any way in this universe

00:25:20.602 --> 00:25:21.733
to get rid of that mass.

00:25:21.733 --> 00:25:24.381
'Cause in the Ant-Man universe, I think

00:25:24.381 --> 00:25:25.943
there's something called a pym particle

00:25:25.943 --> 00:25:29.838
that transfers your mass
out into another dimension.

00:25:29.838 --> 00:25:31.784
- Yeah, well, I mean, the quick answer

00:25:31.784 --> 00:25:34.088
is Einstein taught us that energy and mass

00:25:34.088 --> 00:25:36.229
are actually two sides of the same coin.

00:25:36.229 --> 00:25:38.017
So if you want to change
the mass of something,

00:25:38.017 --> 00:25:40.463
you just extract energy from it.

00:25:40.463 --> 00:25:43.444
So, you could have, you
have Paul Rudd there

00:25:43.444 --> 00:25:45.064
and you just watch heat and light

00:25:45.064 --> 00:25:47.098
and radiation all flying off of him

00:25:47.098 --> 00:25:48.831
as you shrink him down to a smaller size.

00:25:48.831 --> 00:25:49.671
- [Chuck] And then he's on the ant,

00:25:49.671 --> 00:25:51.770
but he's just really tired.

00:25:51.770 --> 00:25:53.723
(laughing)

00:25:53.723 --> 00:25:56.435
- Okay, so if they were to put a nuance

00:25:56.435 --> 00:25:59.505
in the Ant-Man storytelling, he would

00:25:59.505 --> 00:26:02.249
be highly radiant as he got smaller.

00:26:02.249 --> 00:26:03.618
- That would be one way of doing it, yeah.

00:26:03.618 --> 00:26:04.627
- [James] Sounds like you're describing

00:26:04.627 --> 00:26:06.360
a Swanson chicken dinner.

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:07.263
Like in a microwave.

00:26:07.263 --> 00:26:09.513
(laughing)

00:26:10.662 --> 00:26:13.329
I'm just happy to be here, Neil.

00:26:15.201 --> 00:26:17.408
- So, then, so...

00:26:17.408 --> 00:26:19.658
(laughing)

00:26:22.173 --> 00:26:23.757
Does anybody still eat
Swanson chicken dinner?

00:26:23.757 --> 00:26:24.590
Is that what you?

00:26:24.590 --> 00:26:25.471
- I don't know.

00:26:25.471 --> 00:26:28.124
You know, I'm an old geek, I'm sorry.

00:26:28.124 --> 00:26:29.473
- [Neil] But do you have a
girlfriend or somethin', do you?

00:26:29.473 --> 00:26:30.306
- No.

00:26:30.306 --> 00:26:31.139
- [Neil] No, yeah, that's what I'm sayin'.

00:26:31.139 --> 00:26:31.972
Right, right.

00:26:31.972 --> 00:26:33.387
Somebody to eat with, you
just, just a TV dinner.

00:26:33.387 --> 00:26:35.823
You're still eatin', that the thing.

00:26:35.823 --> 00:26:39.143
So, but they described the quantum realm

00:26:39.143 --> 00:26:42.755
and just give me a couple
of minutes reflection

00:26:42.755 --> 00:26:44.488
on the George Gamow series.

00:26:44.488 --> 00:26:46.533
Where he changed the
values of the constants.

00:26:46.533 --> 00:26:47.989
- Oh you mean "Mr. Tompkins Wonderland."

00:26:47.989 --> 00:26:48.891
- Yeah, "Mr. Tompkins Wonderland."

00:26:48.891 --> 00:26:50.285
- Yeah, yeah, that was
from, I think the 1940s

00:26:50.285 --> 00:26:51.118
or something like that.
- Yeah.

00:26:51.118 --> 00:26:53.965
- And, yeah it was a
wonderful series of books

00:26:53.965 --> 00:26:56.717
in which he imagined a universe

00:26:56.717 --> 00:26:59.232
in which somehow human beings are the same

00:26:59.232 --> 00:27:01.502
but the constants of nature
have a different value.

00:27:01.502 --> 00:27:03.850
So, Mr. Tompkins is
doing the kinds of things

00:27:03.850 --> 00:27:04.699
that Paul Rudd is doing, but

00:27:04.699 --> 00:27:06.609
with not quite the same special effects.

00:27:06.609 --> 00:27:08.581
You know, he's there
playing pool and he hits

00:27:08.581 --> 00:27:11.276
the pool ball and the cue
ball passes through one

00:27:11.276 --> 00:27:12.581
of the other balls on the table.

00:27:12.581 --> 00:27:15.342
And it bounces around in a chaotic manner

00:27:15.342 --> 00:27:18.410
that illustrates the many
worlds of quantum physics.

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:20.669
And I think he had a version
where the speed of light

00:27:20.669 --> 00:27:22.548
was like five miles an hour.

00:27:22.548 --> 00:27:25.100
So, then as people are
walking by they're shrunk

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:26.900
and (mumbles) contracted to the size

00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:28.912
of a pancake as they're walking by you.

00:27:28.912 --> 00:27:30.590
So, yeah that was the
kind of early version.

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:32.979
- Just to be an early version
of just imaginary worlds.

00:27:32.979 --> 00:27:33.812
- Exactly.

00:27:33.812 --> 00:27:37.114
- You would easily fold
into a super hero universe.

00:27:37.114 --> 00:27:37.947
- That's right.

00:27:37.947 --> 00:27:38.780
- Yeah, yeah.

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:40.179
- So, ironically, if you were shrunk down,

00:27:40.179 --> 00:27:41.762
you would have less
energy, so you wouldn't

00:27:41.762 --> 00:27:44.858
be more powerful than you
would be at full size, right?

00:27:44.858 --> 00:27:45.826
- [Neil] Oh, good point.

00:27:45.826 --> 00:27:46.745
- So you would be...

00:27:46.745 --> 00:27:49.669
Oh, thank you, I got two, that was two!

00:27:49.669 --> 00:27:52.012
For those who were countin', that's two.

00:27:52.012 --> 00:27:52.928
Jealous?

00:27:52.928 --> 00:27:53.783
- Think about it, right, right.

00:27:53.783 --> 00:27:55.723
Because if you have power as a super hero

00:27:55.723 --> 00:27:57.219
it's in your body somewhere.

00:27:57.219 --> 00:27:59.799
If you gotta release all that
energy to reduce your mass

00:27:59.799 --> 00:28:03.944
to become smaller, you're
a weakling at that point.

00:28:03.944 --> 00:28:05.601
You don't have like the ant strength

00:28:05.601 --> 00:28:07.296
that people always wanna talk about.

00:28:07.296 --> 00:28:08.993
- Yeah, I think it has
to do with the ratio

00:28:08.993 --> 00:28:11.017
of the size to the, I mean you'd have

00:28:11.017 --> 00:28:12.176
to really sort of work it out in detail.

00:28:12.176 --> 00:28:14.721
But yes, in principle, you could be right.

00:28:14.721 --> 00:28:17.100
- [Chuck] Yes, unless
you're Ruth Bader Ginsburg.

00:28:17.100 --> 00:28:19.826
In which case, you have
the power of 10 men.

00:28:19.826 --> 00:28:20.659
No matter what.

00:28:20.659 --> 00:28:24.026
(laughing and cheering)

00:28:24.026 --> 00:28:25.693
- [James] It's true.

00:28:28.234 --> 00:28:30.220
- So, are you familiar with something

00:28:30.220 --> 00:28:32.946
called molecular disequilibrium?

00:28:32.946 --> 00:28:35.079
- No, I don't think I am,
you wanna tell me about that?

00:28:35.079 --> 00:28:36.077
- That's 'cause he just made it up.

00:28:36.077 --> 00:28:38.367
(laughing)

00:28:38.367 --> 00:28:40.187
- Well can you give us a moment,

00:28:40.187 --> 00:28:41.852
tell us about a quantum entanglement.

00:28:41.852 --> 00:28:43.705
- Oh, quantum entanglement, yeah.

00:28:43.705 --> 00:28:45.112
So, this is an idea that, again,

00:28:45.112 --> 00:28:46.334
came from Einstein, that all--

00:28:46.334 --> 00:28:47.645
- Is it only an idea, I
thought we're doin' it.

00:28:47.645 --> 00:28:48.924
- Well that's where it began.

00:28:48.924 --> 00:28:50.940
And Einstein brought it up

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:52.457
because he didn't like quantum mechanics

00:28:52.457 --> 00:28:54.869
and thought this would be the
death nail of quantum physics.

00:28:54.869 --> 00:28:58.914
He pointed out to all of the
worlds quantum mechanicians

00:28:58.914 --> 00:29:00.842
that their theory predicted
that you could have

00:29:00.842 --> 00:29:03.994
a particle over here another
particle over here distant

00:29:03.994 --> 00:29:04.836
from each other.

00:29:04.836 --> 00:29:07.346
But somehow, even though they're separated

00:29:07.346 --> 00:29:09.114
and you do something to this particle,

00:29:09.114 --> 00:29:11.854
the math said that it will
influence that particle,

00:29:11.854 --> 00:29:14.190
regardless of how far
apart they are in space.

00:29:14.190 --> 00:29:15.614
- [Chuck] I have a twin
brother just like that.

00:29:15.614 --> 00:29:17.115
- There you go.

00:29:17.115 --> 00:29:18.926
And Einstein called it spooky.

00:29:18.926 --> 00:29:20.295
The fact that you could do something here

00:29:20.295 --> 00:29:21.128
and effect something there.

00:29:21.128 --> 00:29:23.305
Spooky action at a distance.

00:29:23.305 --> 00:29:24.576
And he thought it was nuts.

00:29:24.576 --> 00:29:26.949
- In the movie there are two characters

00:29:26.949 --> 00:29:28.897
that fuse together from a distance.

00:29:28.897 --> 00:29:31.100
So, there's a quantum thing going on.

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:32.555
But they're microscopic objects.

00:29:32.555 --> 00:29:34.422
You're describing just
subatomic particles.

00:29:34.422 --> 00:29:35.654
- Yeah, I'm talking about particles,

00:29:35.654 --> 00:29:36.599
like electrons and protons.

00:29:36.599 --> 00:29:39.302
But in principle, there's
nothing about the world

00:29:39.302 --> 00:29:41.821
that would prevent these
kinds of entangled qualities

00:29:41.821 --> 00:29:44.131
to exist on arbitrary scales, right?

00:29:44.131 --> 00:29:44.964
- Really?

00:29:44.964 --> 00:29:47.746
- Yeah, it's just hard
to cause the particles

00:29:47.746 --> 00:29:50.240
to maintain those quantum properties.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:51.207
But it's just easier.

00:29:51.207 --> 00:29:52.040
- When you have ensembles of particles.

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:53.946
- Yeah, 'cause they're all
banging into each other,

00:29:53.946 --> 00:29:56.746
knocking each other around and diluting

00:29:56.746 --> 00:29:59.066
the cohesion of the system.

00:29:59.066 --> 00:30:00.535
But you can have it, in principle,

00:30:00.535 --> 00:30:02.265
on arbitrarily large scales.

00:30:02.265 --> 00:30:04.008
- You know what's the
crazy thing is that we,

00:30:04.008 --> 00:30:08.072
in our lifetimes, encryption
will completely change, right?

00:30:08.072 --> 00:30:10.685
We're going to see it
in the next 20, 30 years

00:30:10.685 --> 00:30:12.162
because of quantum entanglement, right?

00:30:12.162 --> 00:30:14.700
Of course, it revolutionized passwords

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:16.492
and how to decrypt things like that.

00:30:16.492 --> 00:30:20.269
But the Russians will still
find ways to hack it, I'm sure.

00:30:20.269 --> 00:30:22.519
(laughing)

00:30:24.397 --> 00:30:25.230
Could you imagine?

00:30:25.230 --> 00:30:26.103
You have
- I'm not fist bumping

00:30:26.103 --> 00:30:27.125
him on that one.

00:30:27.125 --> 00:30:29.890
- You have a quantum entangled password,

00:30:29.890 --> 00:30:33.591
but the password is like
one, two, three, four, five.

00:30:33.591 --> 00:30:36.091
Still, still stupid passwords.

00:30:37.102 --> 00:30:38.545
(laughing)

00:30:38.545 --> 00:30:39.795
- So, so, so...

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:45.600
(laughing)

00:30:48.266 --> 00:30:49.099
So, Brian.

