- And you were in "The Last Mimzy?"

00:51:21.383 --> 00:51:22.963
- I was in "The Last Mimzy."

00:51:22.963 --> 00:51:24.048
- Yes.

00:51:24.048 --> 00:51:25.317
- Yes.

00:51:25.317 --> 00:51:26.762
- You didn't remember
him in "The Last Mimzy?"

00:51:26.762 --> 00:51:27.818
- I'm trying, 'cause I loved that--

00:51:27.818 --> 00:51:29.152
- The little teddy bear had a chip

00:51:29.152 --> 00:51:32.194
in it that was very advanced,
more advanced than anybody.

00:51:32.194 --> 00:51:34.577
And you went in and analyzed the chip.

00:51:34.577 --> 00:51:35.410
That was good.

00:51:35.410 --> 00:51:36.968
- There's nobody who understands that.

00:51:36.968 --> 00:51:37.801
(laughing)

00:51:37.801 --> 00:51:38.634
Thank you.

00:51:38.634 --> 00:51:39.795
But...

00:51:39.795 --> 00:51:40.986
- Yeah.

00:51:40.986 --> 00:51:43.501
- But the actual way I
would describe it is this,

00:51:43.501 --> 00:51:46.675
I'm willing to buy into
a fictional setting

00:51:46.675 --> 00:51:50.171
where whoever it is, the
writer, makes up the rules.

00:51:50.171 --> 00:51:52.556
And if the rules are applied consistently

00:51:52.556 --> 00:51:55.202
and coherently, I'm willing
to go along for the ride.

00:51:55.202 --> 00:51:57.851
What I can't stand is
when partway through,

00:51:57.851 --> 00:52:01.476
the roles change because it
suits whatever narrative--

00:52:01.476 --> 00:52:03.369
- Yeah, I think we all agree on that.

00:52:03.369 --> 00:52:04.243
(audience cheers)

00:52:04.243 --> 00:52:05.708
Right.

00:52:05.708 --> 00:52:08.196
We'll buy your rules, but stay consistent.

00:52:08.196 --> 00:52:09.029
- Yeah.

00:52:09.029 --> 00:52:09.862
- With them.

00:52:09.862 --> 00:52:10.695
- Yeah.

00:52:10.695 --> 00:52:12.412
- Yeah, that works, that works.

00:52:12.412 --> 00:52:14.285
Let me see if I can offer
some parting thoughts.

00:52:14.285 --> 00:52:16.247
I'll do so after, do you
have a final thought here?

00:52:16.247 --> 00:52:18.967
- My final thought is,
I'm not gonna say it,

00:52:18.967 --> 00:52:21.131
I'm just happy to be here.

00:52:21.131 --> 00:52:23.901
But thank you, seriously,
for inviting me, I think--

00:52:23.901 --> 00:52:24.734
- That's not a final thought.

00:52:24.734 --> 00:52:25.567
- No it's not.

00:52:25.567 --> 00:52:26.465
My final thought is this.

00:52:26.465 --> 00:52:29.220
Listening to a lot of the
conversation and thinking about

00:52:29.220 --> 00:52:32.778
the multiverse and then
how inconceivable that is.

00:52:32.778 --> 00:52:36.280
It actually makes you
appreciate how incredible

00:52:36.280 --> 00:52:39.429
and special this exact moment is

00:52:39.429 --> 00:52:41.556
and the exact life you're living is.

00:52:41.556 --> 00:52:43.396
Because it is only here and there's

00:52:43.396 --> 00:52:46.526
a trillion, trillion,
trillion, times other varieties

00:52:46.526 --> 00:52:48.941
of that in existence
according to quantum physics.

00:52:48.941 --> 00:52:50.266
It just makes you really treasure

00:52:50.266 --> 00:52:52.626
and value how amazing
your life is right now.

00:52:52.626 --> 00:52:53.605
- Ooh.

00:52:53.605 --> 00:52:56.538
(audience cheers)

00:52:56.538 --> 00:52:57.371
- Chuck?

00:52:58.839 --> 00:53:00.404
- Murr, that was really cool, man.

00:53:00.404 --> 00:53:01.954
- It was beautiful.

00:53:01.954 --> 00:53:05.871
- Except I'm not--
- It's the weed talking, yes.

00:53:07.384 --> 00:53:10.142
- My final thought on
just the entire evening

00:53:10.142 --> 00:53:12.319
is the fact that isn't it wonderful

00:53:12.319 --> 00:53:15.069
to see that science and curiosity

00:53:16.083 --> 00:53:19.243
has reached a place where
three thousand people

00:53:19.243 --> 00:53:22.258
can convene in New York City in an arena

00:53:22.258 --> 00:53:26.188
to listen to two scientists talk about

00:53:26.188 --> 00:53:30.115
the possibilities and wonder
that is indeed science.

00:53:30.115 --> 00:53:33.588
And because of people
like you, I have faith

00:53:33.588 --> 00:53:37.495
that we may indeed be all right after all.

00:53:37.495 --> 00:53:39.490
(laughing)

00:53:39.490 --> 00:53:41.740
(cheering)

00:53:47.792 --> 00:53:48.625
- Brian?

00:53:48.625 --> 00:53:50.360
- After that, I got nothin' to add, man.

00:53:50.360 --> 00:53:52.475
- You got nothing (laughs).

00:53:52.475 --> 00:53:56.572
So, let me offer some brief reflections.

00:53:56.572 --> 00:53:59.739
It was the 1890s when Percival Lowell,

00:54:01.157 --> 00:54:05.449
a wealthy amateur astronomer,
who had enough money

00:54:05.449 --> 00:54:08.683
to build one of the finest
telescopes ever in the world

00:54:08.683 --> 00:54:10.999
and put it on a mountaintop in Arizona

00:54:10.999 --> 00:54:14.276
and his observatory's
called Lowell Observatory.

00:54:14.276 --> 00:54:17.140
He, among other things,
launched the search

00:54:17.140 --> 00:54:19.207
that discovered Pluto.

00:54:19.207 --> 00:54:21.038
Just so you know.

00:54:21.038 --> 00:54:23.881
(cheering)

00:54:23.881 --> 00:54:25.263
There's some Pluto lovers out there.

00:54:25.263 --> 00:54:26.680
Just get over it.

00:54:28.194 --> 00:54:29.435
Don't get me started.

00:54:29.435 --> 00:54:30.437
(laughing)

00:54:30.437 --> 00:54:32.854
So, too soon, too soon, okay.

00:54:35.017 --> 00:54:38.100
So, he believed he saw canals on Mars

00:54:41.107 --> 00:54:44.024
and it's because he
didn't understand Italian.

00:54:44.024 --> 00:54:47.441
There was an Italian astronomer who wrote

00:54:48.978 --> 00:54:53.978
that he may have seen channels
on Mars with his telescope.

00:54:54.299 --> 00:54:57.877
And the word channel in Italian is canale.

00:54:57.877 --> 00:55:01.109
So he reads this and says,
"Oh, somebody sees canals

00:55:01.109 --> 00:55:02.336
"on Mars, but I have the best telescope

00:55:02.336 --> 00:55:04.060
"at the best location, let me look,

00:55:04.060 --> 00:55:05.692
"I bet I can see them better."

00:55:05.692 --> 00:55:09.823
And so he looks and he thinks
he sees canals on Mars.

00:55:09.823 --> 00:55:12.599
And a channel is different from a canal.

00:55:12.599 --> 00:55:13.505
Why?

00:55:13.505 --> 00:55:14.977
Because a channel is just something

00:55:14.977 --> 00:55:16.246
that running water makes.

00:55:16.246 --> 00:55:20.092
A canal is something
intelligently constructed.

00:55:20.092 --> 00:55:22.706
And he published his books on this.

00:55:22.706 --> 00:55:24.449
First book was titled "Mars."

00:55:24.449 --> 00:55:27.623
Next one "Mars and its Canals."

00:55:27.623 --> 00:55:29.913
And he has maps of the surface of Mars

00:55:29.913 --> 00:55:33.767
with canals connecting nodes to the poles.

00:55:33.767 --> 00:55:37.093
We all know Mars has polar ice caps.

00:55:37.093 --> 00:55:40.449
Even when we will lose ours,
Mars will still have its.

00:55:40.449 --> 00:55:44.781
So, these were canals and he was imagining

00:55:44.781 --> 00:55:46.484
that these were cities that were losing

00:55:46.484 --> 00:55:48.753
their water supply and they were melting

00:55:48.753 --> 00:55:50.138
the ice caps and bringing water

00:55:50.138 --> 00:55:53.293
in this huge irrigation
system, redistributing

00:55:53.293 --> 00:55:54.831
water across its surface.

00:55:54.831 --> 00:55:58.017
This was published in the 1890s.

00:55:58.017 --> 00:56:00.267
Within a few years of this,

00:56:01.635 --> 00:56:04.718
H.G. Wells wrote "War of the Worlds."

00:56:05.921 --> 00:56:07.019
(audience cheers)

00:56:07.019 --> 00:56:09.602
Inspired by those observations.

00:56:10.755 --> 00:56:15.437
If there is life on Mars, I'm
gonna write a story about it.

00:56:15.437 --> 00:56:19.145
I'm gonna take our
imagination to a new place.

00:56:19.145 --> 00:56:21.712
H.G. Wells was scientifically
literate and he had

00:56:21.712 --> 00:56:25.138
the imagination of any brilliant writer,

00:56:25.138 --> 00:56:27.351
that any brilliant writer should.

00:56:27.351 --> 00:56:31.623
So, when I see all of the
storytelling that goes on.

00:56:31.623 --> 00:56:34.830
The stories that we
celebrate here, at Comic Con,

00:56:34.830 --> 00:56:37.834
all dimensions of those
stories, all the creativity

00:56:37.834 --> 00:56:39.712
from the writers, the producers.

00:56:39.712 --> 00:56:42.341
One of the most beautiful
things for me about Comic Con

00:56:42.341 --> 00:56:44.483
is, yes you can bring in the actors,

00:56:44.483 --> 00:56:46.223
but the longest lines are gonna be

00:56:46.223 --> 00:56:49.632
the ones for the writers and
the creative storytellers

00:56:49.632 --> 00:56:52.181
because you know, this audience knows,

00:56:52.181 --> 00:56:55.262
that this is the source
of that creativity.

00:56:55.262 --> 00:56:59.279
And, so here we had just
within a couple of years

00:56:59.279 --> 00:57:03.432
a creative person, a creative person with

00:57:03.432 --> 00:57:06.595
the science literacy,
create this terrifying story

00:57:06.595 --> 00:57:09.053
about martians coming to Earth.

00:57:09.053 --> 00:57:12.519
I do not ever want to see an end

00:57:12.519 --> 00:57:16.935
to the creativity following
the progress of science.

00:57:16.935 --> 00:57:20.951
Giving us no end of stories
to take us into the future,

00:57:20.951 --> 00:57:23.617
so that we can have Comic Cons until all

00:57:23.617 --> 00:57:25.867
the universes may collapse.

00:57:26.827 --> 00:57:28.051
(laughing)

00:57:28.051 --> 00:57:33.051
And that, I'd like to think of
that as a cosmic perspective.

00:57:36.753 --> 00:57:39.096
I want to thank you
all, New York Comic Con,

00:57:39.096 --> 00:57:41.898
for coming out, for Start
Talk at New York Comic Con--

00:57:41.898 --> 00:57:42.731
- [Chuck] Neil.

00:57:42.731 --> 00:57:43.564
- What's that?

00:57:43.564 --> 00:57:44.861
- Before you say goodbye,
because I don't want there

00:57:44.861 --> 00:57:47.803
to be an official end of the show,

00:57:47.803 --> 00:57:52.220
until people recognize that
in just about three hours

00:57:53.883 --> 00:57:58.383
from now, it'll be Dr. Neil
deGrasse Tyson's birthday!

00:57:59.643 --> 00:58:01.893
(cheering)

00:58:04.981 --> 00:58:06.461
- I'm a old man.

00:58:06.461 --> 00:58:09.274
(laughing)

00:58:09.274 --> 00:58:10.107
What?

00:58:10.107 --> 00:58:13.804
- So, so the cool thing
is, the very cool thing

00:58:13.804 --> 00:58:16.299
is we got go do this a few years ago.

00:58:16.299 --> 00:58:18.522
We were on stage on your birthday.

00:58:18.522 --> 00:58:21.937
And it's not officially,
but it is the coolest thing

00:58:21.937 --> 00:58:25.004
in the world to have three thousand people

00:58:25.004 --> 00:58:28.504
actually sing happy birthday to Dr. Tyson.

00:58:30.828 --> 00:58:31.661
So can we,

00:58:32.545 --> 00:58:37.495
♪ Happy birthday to you ♪

00:58:37.495 --> 00:58:42.446
♪ Happy birthday to you ♪

00:58:42.446 --> 00:58:47.446
♪ Happy birthday Dr. Tyson ♪

00:58:49.045 --> 00:58:52.378
♪ Happy birthday to you ♪

00:58:55.091 --> 00:58:56.172
- Thank you.

00:58:56.172 --> 00:58:58.795
(audience cheers)

00:58:58.795 --> 00:58:59.992
Thank you.

00:58:59.992 --> 00:59:02.736
♪ How old are you and the universe ♪

00:59:02.736 --> 00:59:06.549
♪ How old are you and the universe ♪

00:59:06.549 --> 00:59:10.632
- I was born the same week
that NASA was founded.

00:59:12.759 --> 00:59:13.770
The same week.

00:59:13.770 --> 00:59:14.771
(audience cheers)

00:59:14.771 --> 00:59:17.797
And, so NASA and I are the same age.

00:59:17.797 --> 00:59:19.964
We feel each others pains.

00:59:22.170 --> 00:59:27.170
- When NASA looks at Neil it
says, "Neil, I'm your father."

00:59:27.201 --> 00:59:29.451
(laughing)

00:59:31.527 --> 00:59:33.197
- Here's what I wanna do.

00:59:33.197 --> 00:59:34.427
With your permission.

00:59:34.427 --> 00:59:37.576
I wanna end, I'm gonna take
a little more time here,

00:59:37.576 --> 00:59:39.909
of your time, with your permission.

00:59:39.909 --> 00:59:42.742
(audience cheers)

00:59:44.305 --> 00:59:48.472
A few days ago I posted a
birthday letter to NASA.

00:59:50.593 --> 00:59:52.273
An open letter to NASA.

00:59:52.273 --> 00:59:55.377
I wanna read that letter to
you as we end this evening.

00:59:55.377 --> 00:59:56.409
With your permission.

00:59:56.409 --> 00:59:57.242
All right, okay.

00:59:57.242 --> 01:00:00.075
(audience cheers)

01:00:01.833 --> 01:00:04.000
Dear NASA, happy birthday.

01:00:06.197 --> 01:00:10.700
Perhaps you don't know,
but we're the same age.

01:00:10.700 --> 01:00:14.378
In the first week of
October 1958, you were born

01:00:14.378 --> 01:00:17.169
of the National Aeronautics and Space Act

01:00:17.169 --> 01:00:19.652
as a civilian space agency.

01:00:19.652 --> 01:00:22.532
While I was born of my mother.

01:00:22.532 --> 01:00:24.736
(audience laughs)

01:00:24.736 --> 01:00:26.765
In the East Bronx.

01:00:26.765 --> 01:00:30.291
(audience cheers)

01:00:30.291 --> 01:00:31.705
Don't lie to me, there
ain't that many people

01:00:31.705 --> 01:00:33.455
from the Bronx here in
this room right now.

01:00:33.455 --> 01:00:35.392
(audience cheers)

01:00:35.392 --> 01:00:38.016
So, the yearlong celebration

01:00:38.016 --> 01:00:41.781
of our shared 60th anniversary provides me

01:00:41.781 --> 01:00:44.114
a unique occasion to reflect

01:00:45.251 --> 01:00:48.549
on our past, present, and future.

01:00:48.549 --> 01:00:52.940
I was three years old when
John Glenn first orbited Earth.

01:00:52.940 --> 01:00:56.503
I was seven when you lost
astronaut Grissom, Chaffee,

01:00:56.503 --> 01:00:58.942
and White in that tragic fire of

01:00:58.942 --> 01:01:01.861
the Apollo 1 capsule on the launch pad.

01:01:01.861 --> 01:01:06.683
I was 10 when you launched
Armstrong and Aldrin.

01:01:06.683 --> 01:01:09.760
When you landed Armstrong
and Aldrin on the Moon.

01:01:09.760 --> 01:01:14.760
And I was 14 when you stopped
going to the Moon altogether.

01:01:14.836 --> 01:01:19.547
Over that time, I was excited
for you and for America,

01:01:19.547 --> 01:01:22.272
but the vicarious thrill of the journey

01:01:22.272 --> 01:01:24.376
so prevalent in the hearts and minds

01:01:24.376 --> 01:01:27.626
of others, was absent from my emotions.

01:01:28.462 --> 01:01:31.571
I was obviously too
young to be an astronaut.

01:01:31.571 --> 01:01:35.920
But I also knew that my
skin color was much too dark

01:01:35.920 --> 01:01:40.920
for you to picture me as
part of this epic adventure.

01:01:40.921 --> 01:01:44.677
Not only that, even though
you are a civilian agency,

01:01:44.677 --> 01:01:48.775
your most celebrated astronauts
were military pilots,

01:01:48.775 --> 01:01:53.275
at a time when war was
becoming less and less popular.

01:01:55.149 --> 01:01:57.586
During the 1960s, the
Civil Rights movement

01:01:57.586 --> 01:02:01.842
was more real to me than
it surely was to you.

01:02:01.842 --> 01:02:05.039
In fact, you took a directive,
from Vice President Johnson,

01:02:05.039 --> 01:02:09.497
in 1963 to force you
to hire Black engineers

01:02:09.497 --> 01:02:12.451
at your prestigious
Marshall Space Flight Center

01:02:12.451 --> 01:02:14.651
in Huntsville, Alabama.

01:02:14.651 --> 01:02:17.639
I found the correspondence
in your archive.

01:02:17.639 --> 01:02:19.062
Do you remember?

01:02:19.062 --> 01:02:22.332
James Webb, then head of NASA, wrote

01:02:22.332 --> 01:02:25.459
to German rocket pioneer,
Wernher van Braun,

01:02:25.459 --> 01:02:27.826
who headed the center and who was

01:02:27.826 --> 01:02:31.244
the chief engineer of the
entire manned space program.

01:02:31.244 --> 01:02:35.021
The letter boldly and
bluntly directs von Braun

01:02:35.021 --> 01:02:39.193
to address the quote, "Lack of
equal employment opportunity

01:02:39.193 --> 01:02:42.531
"for Negroes in the
region and to collaborate

01:02:42.531 --> 01:02:46.869
"with area colleges, Alabama
A&amp;M and Tuskegee Institute

01:02:46.869 --> 01:02:51.869
"to identify, train, and recruit
qualified Negro engineers

01:02:52.261 --> 01:02:55.094
"into the NASA Huntsville family."

01:02:56.019 --> 01:02:59.436
In 1964, you and I had not yet turned six

01:03:00.722 --> 01:03:03.032
when I saw picketers outside

01:03:03.032 --> 01:03:06.138
the newly built apartment complex

01:03:06.138 --> 01:03:10.763
of our choice in the Riverdale
section of the Bronx.

01:03:10.763 --> 01:03:14.700
They were protesting to
prevent Negro families,

01:03:14.700 --> 01:03:17.496
mine included, from moving there.

01:03:17.496 --> 01:03:19.647
I was glad their effort failed.

01:03:19.647 --> 01:03:22.664
These buildings were called,
perhaps prophetically,

01:03:22.664 --> 01:03:24.747
the "Skyview Apartments,"

01:03:25.713 --> 01:03:28.795
on who's roof, 22 stories over the Bronx,

01:03:28.795 --> 01:03:33.197
I would later train my
telescope on the universe.

01:03:33.197 --> 01:03:36.248
My father was active in
the Civil Rights movement,

01:03:36.248 --> 01:03:38.762
working under New York
City's Mayor Lindsay

01:03:38.762 --> 01:03:42.382
to create job opportunities
for youth in the ghetto,

01:03:42.382 --> 01:03:45.740
as the intercity was called back then.

01:03:45.740 --> 01:03:48.982
Year after year, the
forces operating against

01:03:48.982 --> 01:03:50.815
this effort were huge.

01:03:51.723 --> 01:03:55.213
Poor schools, bad
teachers, meager resources,

01:03:55.213 --> 01:03:58.721
abject racism and assassinated leaders.

01:03:58.721 --> 01:04:01.159
So while you were celebrating
your monthly advances

01:04:01.159 --> 01:04:05.497
in space exploration from
Mercury, to Gemini, to Apollo.

01:04:05.497 --> 01:04:08.790
I was watching America do all it could

01:04:08.790 --> 01:04:11.441
to marginalize who I was and

01:04:11.441 --> 01:04:14.260
what I wanted to become in life.

01:04:14.260 --> 01:04:18.849
I looked to you for guidance,
for a vision statement

01:04:18.849 --> 01:04:21.869
that I could adapt, that
would fuel my ambitions,

01:04:21.869 --> 01:04:24.344
but you weren't there for me.

01:04:24.344 --> 01:04:29.196
Of course, I shouldn't blame
you for society's woes.

01:04:29.196 --> 01:04:30.629
Your conduct was a symptom

01:04:30.629 --> 01:04:33.488
of America's habits, not a cause.

01:04:33.488 --> 01:04:34.891
I knew this.

01:04:34.891 --> 01:04:38.552
But you should nonetheless
know that among my colleagues,

01:04:38.552 --> 01:04:41.711
I'm the only one in my
generation who became

01:04:41.711 --> 01:04:44.913
an astrophysicist in
spite of your achievements

01:04:44.913 --> 01:04:48.060
in space rather than because of them.

01:04:48.060 --> 01:04:52.398
For my inspirations, I
instead turn to libraries,

01:04:52.398 --> 01:04:56.539
remaindered books on the
cosmos from bookstores,

01:04:56.539 --> 01:05:00.622
my rooftop telescope, and
the Hayden Planetarium.

01:05:02.674 --> 01:05:06.189
After some fits and starts
through my years in school,

01:05:06.189 --> 01:05:08.831
where becoming an astrophysicist seemed

01:05:08.831 --> 01:05:11.712
at times to be the path of most resistance

01:05:11.712 --> 01:05:14.172
through an unwelcoming society,

01:05:14.172 --> 01:05:17.382
I became a professional astrophysicist,

01:05:17.382 --> 01:05:19.749
I became a scientist.

01:05:19.749 --> 01:05:24.294
Over the decades that followed,
you've come a long way.

01:05:24.294 --> 01:05:26.109
Whoever does not yet
recognize the value of

01:05:26.109 --> 01:05:29.802
this adventure to our
Nation's future, soon will,

01:05:29.802 --> 01:05:31.211
as the rest of the development

01:05:31.211 --> 01:05:34.107
and developing world passes us by

01:05:34.107 --> 01:05:39.107
and every measure of technological
leadership and strength.

01:05:39.589 --> 01:05:44.237
Not only that, these days you
look much more like America.

01:05:44.237 --> 01:05:45.807
From your senior level managers

01:05:45.807 --> 01:05:48.106
to your most decorated astronauts.

01:05:48.106 --> 01:05:52.539
Congratulations, you now
belong to the entire citizenry.

01:05:52.539 --> 01:05:55.254
Examples of this are bound,
but I especially remember

01:05:55.254 --> 01:05:58.363
when the public took ownership
of the Hubble Telescope,

01:05:58.363 --> 01:06:01.269
your most beloved unmanned mission.

01:06:01.269 --> 01:06:04.506
They all spoke loudly, back in 2004,

01:06:04.506 --> 01:06:06.481
ultimately reversing the threat

01:06:06.481 --> 01:06:09.815
that the telescope might not
be serviced a fourth time.

01:06:09.815 --> 01:06:12.700
Extending its life for another decade.

01:06:12.700 --> 01:06:15.344
Hubble's transcended images of the cosmos

01:06:15.344 --> 01:06:17.248
had spoken to us all.

01:06:17.248 --> 01:06:21.177
As did the personal profiles
of the space shuttle astronauts

01:06:21.177 --> 01:06:24.245
who deployed and serviced the telescope.

01:06:24.245 --> 01:06:28.222
And the scientists who
benefited from its data stream.

01:06:28.222 --> 01:06:31.149
Not only that, I had even joined

01:06:31.149 --> 01:06:35.257
the ranks of your most
trusted as I served dutifully

01:06:35.257 --> 01:06:38.025
on your prestigious Advisory Council.

01:06:38.025 --> 01:06:40.756
I came to recognize that
when you're at your best,

01:06:40.756 --> 01:06:42.339
nothing in this world can inspire

01:06:42.339 --> 01:06:45.589
the dreams of a nation the way you can.

01:06:50.331 --> 01:06:52.081
Dreams fueled by a...

01:06:57.632 --> 01:07:00.993
A pipeline of ambitious students,

01:07:00.993 --> 01:07:05.660
eager to become scientists,
engineers, and technologists

01:07:10.950 --> 01:07:15.283
in the service of the
greatest quest there ever was.

01:07:18.813 --> 01:07:20.209
Sorry, sorry.

01:07:20.209 --> 01:07:23.209
(audience applauds)

01:07:33.003 --> 01:07:37.087
You have come to represent
a fundamental part

01:07:37.087 --> 01:07:42.004
of America's identity, not only
to itself but to the world.

01:07:46.379 --> 01:07:48.962
So as we both turn 60 and begin

01:07:50.246 --> 01:07:54.246
our 61st trip around the
sun, I want you to know

01:07:55.805 --> 01:07:59.388
that I feel your pains
and share your joys.

01:08:02.087 --> 01:08:06.254
And I look forward to
seeing you back on the Moon.

01:08:07.741 --> 01:08:09.692
But don't stop there.

01:08:09.692 --> 01:08:13.025
Mars beckons, as do destinations beyond.

01:08:16.055 --> 01:08:17.305
Birthday buddy.

01:08:20.720 --> 01:08:23.137
Even if I've not always been.

01:08:25.303 --> 01:08:28.311
I'm now your humble servant.

01:08:28.311 --> 01:08:30.188
Neil deGrasse Tyson.

01:08:30.188 --> 01:08:31.128
Thank you all.

01:08:31.128 --> 01:08:33.961
(audience cheers)

01:08:38.605 --> 01:08:39.438
Thank you.

01:08:44.040 --> 01:08:47.067
- Dr. Neil deGrasse Tyson,
ladies and gentleman.

01:08:47.067 --> 01:08:49.067
Dr. Neil deGrasse Tyson.

01:08:51.466 --> 01:08:53.531
- And our panel.

01:08:53.531 --> 01:08:57.781
- And give it up for the
Murr, James "Murr" Murray.

01:08:58.884 --> 01:09:01.801
Theoretical physicist Brian Greene.

01:09:05.691 --> 01:09:06.723
My name is Chuck Nice.

01:09:06.723 --> 01:09:09.358
Thank you, and as always, we leave you

01:09:09.358 --> 01:09:11.719
with Dr. Tyson saying...

01:09:11.719 --> 01:09:13.219
- Keep lookin' up.

